  "question": {
    "text": "A heavy nucleus&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>&nbsp;of half-life&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>20</mn></math>&nbsp;minutes undergoes alpha-decay with probability of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp; and beta-decay with probability of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>40</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. Initially, the number of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>&nbsp;nuclei is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1000</mn></math>. The number of alpha-decays of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Q</mi></math>&nbsp;in the first one hour is:"
  },
